    citation: "(1584) 76 ER 637 & AIR 1955 SC 661 (7-Judge Bench)",
    passageText: `The primary and golden rule of statutory interpretation is that the words of a statute must prima facie be given their natural, ordinary, and grammatical meaning (Litera legis / Literal Rule), for the Legislature is presumed to have meant what it has plainly expressed. Only where the grammatical construction leads to an manifest absurdity, repugnance, or inconsistency with the rest of the instrument may the grammatical and ordinary sense of the words be modified just so far as is necessary to avoid that absurdity and no further (the 'Golden Rule' articulated by Lord Wensleydale in Grey v. Pearson, 1857).

Where a statute is remedial or enacted to cure a specific defect in the pre-existing law, Courts apply the 'Mischief Rule' (or Purposive Construction) originating in [1] and applied by a seven-Judge Bench of the Supreme Court in [2] even to the interpretation of Article 286 of the Constitution. Alongside these primary rules stand established subsidiary Latin maxims of construction: Noscitur a sociis (the meaning of a doubtful word may be ascertained by reference to the meaning of words associated with it), Ejusdem generis (where specific words forming a distinct genus or category are followed by general words, the general words are restricted to things of the same kind as those specified), and Expressio unius est exclusio alterius (the express mention of one thing implies the exclusion of another).`,
